# Optimal compute (generated by `computeplan rules --write`; edit the skill, not this file)

No job runs on any tier until it passes that tier's check. This is a hard rule from the user
(2026-10-18, extended 2026-10-20). Competition priority is CASMI > NTL > bdb27, but that is **advice
only**: the user decides which job runs next from results and checkpoints.

## Tier order: laptop -> Colab L4 -> Kaggle T4 x2 -> rented

| Tier | Hardware | Budget | Use it for |
|---|---|---|---|
| laptop | RTX 3050 **4 GB**, 16 threads, ~20 GB RAM (WSL) | free | smoke tests, CPU work, anything that fits 4 GB |
| **Colab L4** | L4 **23 GB**, 12 vCPU, 55 GB RAM, 1.54 CU/h | ~100 CU/month (measured 160 CU = ~104 L4-h) | the default for single-GPU training |
| Colab T4 | T4 15 GB, **2 vCPU**, 1.07 CU/h | same pool | only with a stated reason: 2 vCPUs starve a dataloader |
| Kaggle | T4 x2 or P100, 4 vCPU, 29 GB; TPU v3-8 separate | **45 GPU-h/week**, resets Sat 00:00 UTC | submission notebooks, true dual-GPU stages, TPU |
| rented | - | $0 cap, no provider | nothing: refused until a bottleneck is measured on all three free tiers |

Colab A100 and G4 were unavailable and H100 is unentitled on this account. A job needing more than
23 GB escalates to rented, not to Colab.

## The commands

```bash
computeplan init-plan DIR --target laptop|colab|kaggle-gpu|rented
computeplan check DIR          # CHECKPOINT 1: lint + stamp. A later edit voids the stamp.
computeplan launch DIR         # runs it; --wait queues behind the laptop GPU lock
computeplan verify DIR         # CHECKPOINT 3: measured use vs the plan, one ledger row
computeplan board --watch      # what is running and queued on every tier, with ETAs
computeplan usage              # every tier's remaining budget
```

Never call `colab new|run|exec` or `kaggle kernels push` directly: the launchers refuse, because those
spend compute units or the weekly quota. Read-only subcommands are open.

## What each gate refuses

- **laptop**: no stamp, stale stamp, `vram_peak_gb_est` over 3.6 GB on a 4 GB card, missing
  `memory_max`, `gpu: true` with no GPU code, or another job holding the GPU lock.
- **Colab**: accelerator outside cpu/T4/L4, T4 without a reason, over the 12 h session cap, missing or
  exceeded CU cap, estimate over the live balance, GPU runtime with no GPU code, **any keepalive or
  anti-idle trick** (breaches Colab terms and would cost 15 Kaggle GPU-h/week), no incremental save
  against the ~90 min idle disconnect, or unsynced code.
- **Kaggle**: the existing E/W/P codes in `kaggle-gpu-push`; a push only through that tool.
- **rented**: everything above plus P20 (measured bottleneck at laptop, Colab and Kaggle), a USD cap
  per run and per month, a teardown rule, and the cost typed back with `--confirm-usd`.

## P21: what runs is what GitHub shows

Code reaches Colab and rented VMs as the tarball of the **synced repo folder**, never with a credential.
A launch is refused when `codesync status <project>` is not clean, the folder is dirty, or commits are
unpushed. Push code with `codesync push <project>`; only code files travel (path allowlist, 2 MB cap,
secret scan). Live competitions live in the private `avndope/kaggle-live`; finished ones in the public
`avndope/Kaggle_Competitions`.

## Local facts that have already cost time

- `nvidia-smi` **blocks under WSL** while the GPU is initialising or saturated: one query timed out
  after 20 s. Never poll it without a timeout, and treat a stall as "busy".
- A GPU job must print its own peak VRAM (`torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`); the gates read that line
  rather than trusting a sampler.
- One GPU job at a time on the laptop, enforced by an flock. Queue with `computeplan launch DIR --wait`.
- Kaggle's exact weekly hours exist only in account settings; record them with
  `computeplan quota --kaggle-left H` when you see them.

